{"title":"Wind farm area polygon, viewshed, and impact","markdown":{"headingText":"Wind farm area polygon, viewshed, and impact","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-e06","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n> **Dates of practical:** 20th and 27th November and 11th December 2025\n\nThis exercise assumes that you have followed Exercises 04 and 05, created wind farm planning constraints for a 20 × 20 km map tile in Wales (protected areas, transport, housing, water, topography), and investigated wind speed. Data are on the **L:** drive under `College of Science/Geography/GEG276`. Read alongside **GIS_guide_to_practical_exercises** and the wind farm Project instructions on Canvas.\n\n::: {.callout-note}\n## Learning aims\n\nCreate and edit a polygon to represent a wind farm, calculate its area, create a viewshed, and count the population living within view of the proposed wind farm.\n:::\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Starting point\n\n(@) Prepare a QGIS project including vector constraint layers and a wind speed raster. From Maps 2 and 3, choose the single optimum unconstrained location in your tile for a wind farm (sufficiently large, with high wind). Verify suitability with OpenStreetMap XYZ Tiles. Use the Measure tool to confirm a narrowest dimension > 150 m.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> wind farm polygon creation\n\n(@) Create a new GeoPackage polygon layer (**Layer → Create Layer → New GeoPackage Layer**). Choose a sensible name and location, **Geometry type** = polygon, **CRS** = EPSG:27700. Right‑click the layer and **Toggle Editing**.\n\n(@) Add a polygon feature (**Edit → Add Polygon Feature**). Left‑click to draw vertices, right‑click to finish, and enter an ID (e.g., 1). Use the mouse wheel/middle button to navigate as you edit.\n\n(@) Improve the polygon shape with editing tools as needed (e.g., **Vertex Tool**).\n\n(@) Toggle off editing and save changes.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> How big is your wind farm?\n\n(@) Calculate polygon area using the **Field Calculator**. Create a new field `area` (Decimal/real) and use `$area`. Open the attribute table to read the area (m²) and convert to km². A 2 MW turbine needs ~0.5 km². Estimate potential turbine count. Ensure narrowest width ≥ 150 m to be viable.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Creating a viewshed\n\n(@) Install the **Visibility Analysis** plugin.\n\n(@) Create a centroid point for your wind farm polygon (**Vector → Geometry Tools → Centroids**). This point represents the wind farm location for viewshed analysis.\n\n(@) In the **Processing Toolbox**, run **Visibility Analysis → Create Viewpoints**. Set Observer locations = wind farm centroid; DEM = your tile DEM; Radius of analysis = 10 km; Observer height = 50 m; Target height = 2 m. Save the output viewpoint layer.\n\n(@) Run **Visibility Analysis → Analysis → Viewshed** using the viewpoint layer and your tile DEM. Explore and understand the result.\n\n## <input type=\"checkbox\"> Calculating impact\n\n(@) Vectorise your viewshed (see Exercise 05) to create polygons representing areas within 10 km from which the wind farm is visible. Use select‑by‑value and save selected features.\n\n(@) Using methods from Exercise 03, calculate the total number of people registered at postcodes within line‑of‑sight. If errors about “invalid geometry” occur, buffer the visibility polygon by 1 m and retry.\n\n(@) Design **Map 4** to present the wind farm location/extent and its visual impact. Focus on population and visibility; you do not need to repeat earlier context maps.\n\n(@) Comment in your CW4 caption on how well your analysis captures potential impact if your wind farm lies near the tile edge.\n\n::: {.callout-tip}\n# Learning outcomes\n\nBy the end of this exercise you should:\n\n1. Create and edit a vector layer in QGIS.\n2. Create a new polygon layer to represent a proposed wind farm location.\n3. Calculate the area of a vector polygon and estimate turbine capacity.\n4. Create a viewshed using an observer/target and a DEM.\n5. Quantify population within sight of the proposed wind farm and its proportion of the tile population.\n:::\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","output-file":"e06.html"},"language":{"toc-title-document":"Table of contents","toc-title-website":"On this page","related-formats-title":"Other Formats","related-notebooks-title":"Notebooks","source-notebooks-prefix":"Source","other-links-title":"Other Links","code-links-title":"Code Links","launch-dev-container-title":"Launch Dev Container","launch-binder-title":"Launch Binder","article-notebook-label":"Article Notebook","notebook-preview-download":"Download Notebook",